    "## Spatial Alignment of Sentinel Features to Training and Test Samples\n",
    "After cleaning and finalizing the building-level Sentinel feature dataset, the next step was to spatially align these features with the training and test samples using a nearest-neighbor join, following the same conceptual approach previously used for Landsat-derived features.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Initial Nearest-Neighbor Join Attempt\n",
    "Sentinel features were first converted back into a point-based GeoDataFrame using the centroid coordinates encoded in the `building_key`. A nearest spatial join was then applied between the Sentinel feature points and the train/test locations, using a maximum distance threshold consistent with the feature extraction buffer (200 m).\n",
    "\n",
    "At face value, this join appeared successful: **100% of training and test samples were matched**. However, distance diagnostics revealed a critical issue. Despite specifying a distance constraint, the matched Sentinel points were often **several kilometers away**, with mean match distances exceeding **1.8 km**, far larger than both the intended buffer radius and the distances observed in the Landsat pipeline.\n",
    "\n",
    "This discrepancy indicated that the spatial join was **not honoring the distance constraint**, despite appearing syntactically correct.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Root Cause: Version-Specific Behavior in GeoPandas\n",
    "Further investigation confirmed that the issue was not due to coordinate system mismatch or geographic misalignment—the Sentinel and train/test datasets clearly overlapped spatially. Instead, the root cause was traced to a **version limitation in GeoPandas (v1.1.1)**.\n",
    "\n",
    "In this version, the `max_distance` parameter in `sjoin_nearest` is silently ignored. As a result, the join degenerates into an *unbounded nearest-neighbor match*, guaranteeing a match for every point regardless of distance. This behavior explains the misleading 100% match rate coupled with implausibly large distances.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Final Join Strategy (Adopted Approach)\n",
    "To resolve this, the join strategy was revised as follows:\n",
    "1. **Perform an unconstrained nearest-neighbor join**, explicitly requesting the raw distance output from `sjoin_nearest`.\n",
    "2. **Convert distances from degrees to meters**, enabling meaningful physical interpretation.\n",
    "3. **Apply a manual distance threshold in meters**, consistent with the Sentinel feature extraction scale.\n",
    "4. **Retain distance metadata** for transparency and downstream quality control.\n",
    "\n",
    "**UPDATE:** This approach was further refined by **reprojecting all data to a meter-based UTM coordinate system (EPSG:32618)** before performing the spatial join. This eliminated the need for approximate degree-to-meter conversion and provided accurate distance measurements directly from the spatial join.\n",
    "\n",
    "This approach restores full control over spatial validity while remaining compatible with the installed library version.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Distance Distribution and Threshold Selection\n",
    "The resulting distance distributions were highly informative and demonstrated excellent spatial coherence:\n",
    "* **Minimum match distance**: 2.5 m (indicating precise centroid matching)\n",
    "* **Median match distance**: 46.4 m\n",
    "* **Mean match distance**: 56.8 m\n",
    "* **Maximum match distance**: 303.5 m\n",
    "* **90% of samples** matched within ~112 m\n",
    "* **95% of samples** matched within ~128 m\n",
    "\n",
    "These statistics confirm that the nearest Sentinel building centroids are typically located within 50 meters of our sample points, with 95% within 128 meters. This tight spatial distribution validates the building-level feature extraction approach and closely mirrors the behavior observed in the Landsat pipeline.\n",
    "\n",
    "Based on this distribution, a **200 m threshold** was selected as the final cutoff:\n",
    "* It retains **98.4% of training samples and 98.2% of test samples** while maintaining strong spatial fidelity.\n",
    "* A more conservative 100 m threshold would have retained ~86% of samples, but at the cost of potentially excluding relevant nearby features.\n",
    "* The 200 m threshold aligns perfectly with the original feature extraction buffer radius used during Sentinel feature computation.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Impact on Dataset Size\n",
    "Applying the 200 m distance threshold resulted in a **minimal and intentional reduction in sample size**:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Dataset | Original Size | Final Size | Retention Rate |\n",
    "| ------- | ------------- | ---------- | -------------- |\n",
    "| Train   | 8,982         | 8,834      | 98.4%          |\n",
    "| Test    | 2,246         | 2,205      | 98.2%          |\n",
    "\n",
    "The small loss (~1.6-1.8%) reflects cases where no Sentinel building centroid lay sufficiently close to the sample location. Rather than forcing low-quality matches, these samples were excluded to preserve spatial integrity.\n",
    "\n",
    "Importantly, this reduction is **not random**: it removes only those observations for which Sentinel context cannot be reliably assigned at the intended spatial scale.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Final Output\n",
    "The final training and test datasets include:\n",
    "* All original non-leaking features,\n",
    "* Six building-scale Sentinel features,\n",
    "* **No geographic metadata or distance columns** (these were intentionally removed to prevent data leakage)\n",
    "\n",
    "**Final Output:**\n",
    "- Training dataset: **8,834 samples × 30 features**\n",
    "- Test dataset: **2,205 samples × 30 features**\n",
    "- All 6 Sentinel features successfully integrated\n",
    "- **No missing values** in final datasets\n",
    "- **No geographic/distance columns** remaining (preventing data leakage)\n",
    "\n",
    "The process successfully integrates Sentinel-derived building characteristics while ensuring spatial coherence and preventing data leakage, resulting in datasets ready for robust machine learning model development. The high retention rate (98%+) with tight distance distribution provides strong evidence that the spatial alignment is accurate and that Sentinel features are assigned from appropriately proximal buildings."
